  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A company manufactures electronic components. The components are produced by two machines, $A$ and $B.$ In a particular random sample, $37$ of the components sampled were produced by machine $A,$ and $43$ were produced by machine $B.$ On inspecting the sampled components, it was found that $5$ defective components were produced by machine $A$ and $3$ defective components were produced by machine $B.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the event that the chosen component was produced by machine $A$, and let $D$ be the event that the chosen component is defective. Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap {A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(D\\cap A),$ we take the number of defective components produced by machine $A,$ and divide by the total number of defective components $(37+43=80).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap A)=\\frac{5}{80}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(D\\cap {A}^{\u2032}),$ we take the number of defective components produced by machine $B,$ and divide by the total number of defective components $(37+43=80).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap {A}^{\u2032})=\\frac{3}{80}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap {A}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{80}+\\frac{3}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
